Package: cpumon
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: scrollable, man-page-styled Linux CPU usage monitor
 cpumon reads /proc/stat (aggregate and per-core user/nice/system/idle/
 iowait/irq/softirq/steal jiffie counters, context switches, forks,
 running/blocked process counts) along with /proc/loadavg, /proc/uptime,
 /proc/cpuinfo, CPU pressure stall information, per-core frequency, CPU
 temperature sensors and per-process CPU time, and displays it as a
 scrollable, man-page-styled terminal report with usage history
 sparklines and a top-processes list, auto-refreshing on a configurable
 interval (seconds, minutes, hours, or days).
 .
 It also runs headless via a bundled systemd service (enabled and started
 automatically on install), appending timestamped snapshots to a daily
 log file as text, JSON Lines or CSV, and/or to the systemd journal, with
 optional high-usage alerts. Old log files are gzip-compressed and pruned
 after configurable periods (see /etc/cpumon/cpumon.conf), and
 `cpumon --replay` prints logged history back out.
